o  in there so at this point I'm just  printing stuff it's pretty unexcited but  yeah this is just to give you a feeling  for how similar it is to observables  really you just subscribe to these  certain events and you get going you can  also create streams in a few different  ways you have your  stream constructors so you know this is  like if you want to create a stream from  an iterable or a future which is a nice  async primitive that's also baked into  Dart if you want to get more advanced  you can do concatenation or deferring  streams with rx start and there's a  quite a few of other more operators or  constructors that are useful but this is  just to give you a sample the other cool  thing as I said is you get language  level support for streams in dart and  this is a really interesting pattern  actually I think so  in this case we've got a function called  search and it takes in the search term  and the form of a string and what we're  gonna do is we're gonna mark this  function as being an async star function  which means that we're required to  return some type of stream and so in  this case I'm just gonna make the  example really silly and say okay we're  returning a string but I think you could  imagine you could return more complex or  more interesting objects from this type  of function and the cool thing is that  once you're inside this function alright  so once you're inside this function you  could actually use the yield keyword  nice it's working again so every time  you yield a value it will be emitted to  the listeners of this stream and this is  a really nice way so you can write kind  of maybe a little bit less like crazy rx  code and write maybe a little more  imperative code so this search what  we'll want to do is we'll want to emit a  loading event so that our you I can show  loading and then within a try-catch  block what we can do is we can actually  say hey let's go ahead and try to call  our search service and assume it returns  a future and we'll just wait for that  future to complete and if it completes  successfully will yield that value out  to the listeners of the stream but if it  fails for some reason we can emit an  error event so you know I think you  could think if you've worked with rx you  know I think you could model this in the  same way with rx very easily you know  you could you know do a start with  loading event and then you know on error  return you know the the error instead  but I think async functions are kind of  a nice way to clean that up actually and  the final way is with something called a  stream controller which is sort of glue  but  Queen the inputs and then outputs of a  stream so it provides a stream that you  can listen to and then you can also it  also provides what's called an events  sync and this allows you to add data to  the stream or errors or tell the stream  that hey this actually is done now and  so yeah so you've got these three  methods basically on the event sink add  a tear and close and those will of  course get sent to the places you would  expect the listener to receive them so  yeah so that's really nice and if you're  used to like rx observers so you're  working with maybe like a subject it's  almost the identical right you can kind  of see that ad sort of corresponds to on  next add error risk corresponds to on  error and close corresponds to the on  complete so this brings us back to the  block pattern that we were discussing so  as I said these stands for business  logic components and they've got a few  special rules and so this is really the  reason I was kind of describing stream  controllers especially is that sinks are  used for inputs so the view layer can  add data to a sink and then the block  can listen to the stream of that input  and react to it and then the block will  also send streams of data back to the  view layer and then the view layer can  actually subscribe to those streams and  update the state when that change or  update the view excuse me when that  stream changes and because this is meant  to be used cross-platform between  angulardart  and also flutter apps it's really  important that you don't inject any  dependencies like concrete  implementations but you inject abstract  in interfaces or abstract classes and  the reason you want to do that is  because if you're in the browser context  you can you know use like a browser HTTP  client if you're in flutter you can use  what's called the i/o client or if  you're in a test scenario you could  inject a mock client for example so if  we look at a basic block we'll just call  it a counter block and you know it'll  actually emit so if this is basically  the same thing as we had initially so  it'll emit a stream of integers that  we'll call a counter and then to get  data from the view layer it'll provide a  sink  and this is just called the increments  Inc and just to be good citizens you  definitely generally want to dispose of  your sinks when you're done with them so  say for example you have your showing a  view or showing a screen and then that  screen should go away you'll definitely  want to clean up after yourself so yeah  we'll just close off the sink when we're  done here and then what you can do is  under the hood actually the block can  use either less stream controller which  I just talked about or in this case I'm  going to use a published subject which  comes from rx start and actually  implements the stream controller  interface and the only reason I'm doing  that is because it gives you all of the  RX operators by default rather than  having to convert a stream into an  observable and then use those things so  yeah so we'll create a subject here and  then since we don't want to expose the  full subject to the view layer we'll  just and the subject I should say also  implements the sink interface so we'll  say ok we'll provide a sink to the view  layer by just basically aliasing subject  and reducing some of its capabilities  and in the counter stream we'll just  basically listen to the subject whenever  someone taps on that button and it'll  just run an accumulator function so in  this case we're using the scan keyword  and we're just adding to the previous  count every time a new tap comes in or  every time the subject emits some sort  of item so the cool thing is that these  are actually really testable so dart as  I said because streams are baked in you  actually get support with the testing  library as well so you can this is just  a trivial example but I think it shows  actually kind of how nice some of the  testing is so you can just say ok I've  got this stream and I expect it to emit  these values in order and so yeah if  you're worried about maybe your UI  emitting States in the wrong way you can  really easily test like hey is my stream  doing the right thing in the right order  you can actually also test errors as  well so you can say hey does this  observable or this stream does it emit  an error of a certain type or you know  you could provide more complex logic in  here this is exception is just a just a  matcher and you can write your own  matchers  to verify that you're getting the right  thing  and so we've talked a little bit about  the sort of block or the presenter layer  so what about flutter then like where  does it come into the picture well we  talked about it really being the view  layer and so what it can do is it can  actually render the data from a stream  using a class widget called a stream  builder and so this actually takes in  two parameters a stream and a builder  it's a stream builder  yeah what it says on the box so in this  case we'll pass through the counter  stream to this widget and then the  Builder function will actually return a  widget that's built up so you basically  take in the data that the stream emits  and you render a widget using that data  so this is really cool because then you  know whenever it changes you can just  create a new widget hierarchy and  flutter will take care of rendering that  to the screen you don't really need to  think about setting up subscriptions or  do any doing anything like that the  other thing is we can do is of course we  want to be able to send data back to the  block so that it can run that  accumulator function and increase the  count and so what we'll do is we'll say  okay since we've got a sink that we can  use we'll just add data to the sink yeah  when it's when it's ready to go so I  think what we can do is we can kind of  look at our check list again and we can  start to see that we're layering our  architectures in a better way it's more  modular it's definitely much more  testable then then we saw before I we  couldn't we didn't get into all of that  but you can definitely see how testable  it is we can definitely follow the solid  principles here we're segregating  responsibilities we're injecting  abstract interfaces rather than concrete  classes etc and of course you can do all  the normal stuff you can still talk to  web api's this is a really smaller small  example but you can definitely do all  the all the powerful things so yeah my  conclusion from this is that first of  all there's no reason for us you know I  really like flutter and I think it's  really fun but there's no reason for us  to like reinvent the architectural wheel  in some ways like we have really good  patterns that were used to from Android  that work well for statically typed  languages and we can actually use all of  these normal patterns that we've used in  the past and combine them with some  the things that dart offers out of the  box like streams and we can combine  these to make really powerful  architectures that are easy to test easy  to work with and also allow us to share  a great deal of code between even  angulardart apps if you want to go that  route so yeah I'll post these slides  afterwards but to get look at more  advanced examples I've got some  different examples up here some of these  come from the flutter team other the  other are ones that I've done there's  also more talks on the block pattern  itself so yeah once again you can check  these links out and thank you very much  thanks you Brian  [Applause]
 

